# Working in this application

Generated by the Dataspecer application generator from an application graph. Read `README.md` first
for the layout. This file covers the rules that are not obvious from the code.

## Before you change anything

Regeneration overwrites every file, including this one. A change meant to be permanent belongs in
the application graph, or in the generator that reads it. Changes made here are kept across a
regeneration by merging them into the new output, which is why the README names the places designed
for them.

`src/shared/` is copied unchanged into every generated application. Nothing in it imports from
`src/config/` or `src/modules/`, and that has to hold: those directories exist only in a generated
application, so an import of one would not resolve anywhere else the library is used.

## Extension points, in the order you should reach for them

1. **A page's behaviour**: `src/modules/<name>/<route>-operation.ts`. Each class extends a default
   strategy. `invokeOperation` in `src/shared/operations/operation-strategy.ts` runs
   `validateRequest`, then `execute`, then `postprocess`. Validation issues stop the sequence and
   come back as a failed result, so `execute` never runs. Both hooks are optional and an absent one
   is skipped. Everything they receive is on `OperationContext`: the aggregate descriptor, the
   registry, the data source, the route params and the payload. Everything they return is an
   `OperationResult`, which is either `{ ok: true, data }` or `{ ok: false, issues }`.
2. **The data source**: `src/config/data-sources.ts`. Implement the `DataSource` interface from
   `src/shared/data-source/data-source.ts` and hand your instance to `DataSourceProvider` in
   `main.tsx`. Components read it through `useDataSource()`, so nothing else changes. The interface
   is the whole contract: reads take an aggregate descriptor and return entities, `readList` also
   takes `page`, `pageSize` and `sort` and returns `items` with a `total`, and writes take the
   entity the forms produced. `listIncomingReferences` backs the warning on the delete page.
3. **The look**: `src/theme.ts`. Every component takes its colours, spacing and density from there.
4. **A shared component**: `src/shared/components/`. Changing one changes every page that uses it.

## Conventions

Filenames are kebab-case. One folder per data structure. A page holds its own title, navigation and
strategy, so a graph change shows up in the page it affects.

`@/` resolves to `src/`. Imports that cross an area use it. Imports between siblings and inside
`src/shared/` stay relative.

Entity identifiers are IRIs, not opaque ids. `src/shared/forms/iri.ts` rejects the characters SPARQL
forbids, which is stricter than a URI needs to be, because the default data source writes SPARQL.

Dates are `Date` objects in the models and Luxon values in the pickers. The conversion is in
`form-field.tsx`.

## Field metadata

`src/modules/<name>/descriptor.ts` is what the forms, the lists and the detail views read. A field
carries its `path` (used in validation paths and grid columns), its `propertyName` (used in the
model and in the RDF mapping), its label, its kind, its cardinality as `minCount` and `maxCount`,
and for a primitive the `formControl` to render. An association also carries the IRI of the target
class and, for a composition, the nested fields. Reading a descriptor tells you what a page will
render without running it.

## Things that will bite you

Forms carry `noValidate`. The application validates and reports problems itself, and the browser's
own validation would block submit before that runs.

`aggregateRegistry` is keyed by aggregate IRI. Field metadata refers to other structures by that
IRI, so resolving a composition or a reference target goes through it.

The generated model types are stricter than the reads. A field marked required is typed
non-optional, while the RDF schema marks every property optional, so a missing triple gives
`undefined` where the type promises a value. Guard before calling methods on a read value.

Composite writes are not transactional. Leaves are written before roots, and a failure part-way
leaves the earlier writes in place.

## Verifying a change

```sh
npm run lint      # type-aware, and hook dependencies are errors here
npm run build     # tsc, then the bundle
npm run dev       # then click the path you changed
```

Run all three. `tsc` and the lint cover different ground: `react-hooks/exhaustive-deps` is an error
here, and a wrong dependency list is invisible to the compiler.
